  "summary": [
    "Batch A, family A10 (supplied-family incidence degree). GTBase.incidence.incidence_degree F v := #|[set e in F | v \\in e]| counts the members of an ARBITRARY finite family on an arbitrary finType; no edge, uniformity, nonemptiness or palette premise is added. Six corpus copies (X36 x36_degree_in_edge_set, X84 x84_degree_in, X37/X38 xNN_subgraph_degree, X6 x6_hg_degree, X73 x73_hyperdegree) and the two explicitly enrolled public sources (Hypergraph.foundations.hypergraph.hg_degree in its Section context, ClassicalLemmas.konig.line_colouring.edeg) convert to it. X108 x108_degree_in E W v keeps its explicit inside-W restriction: it equals incidence_degree [set e in E | e \\subset W] v (a proved equality, conjunction order), zero for v outside W. GTBase.common.incidence_degree_edges gives #|N(v)| at E(G).",
    "",
    "Ten statements are affected through fourteen chains: X36, X84 (cycle edge sets, count, cycle length), X85 (X84's count, across files), X37, X38, X6, XE2 #833 (X6's degree, across files), X73 (regularity), X108 (d-degeneracy) and X225 (the foundation's degeneracy test, opaque witness, least degeneracy, skeletal degeneracy and d_max). FoundationLegacy keeps the original Section context; the witness's two support lemmas hg_degree_le and hg_degenerate_card are copied verbatim, statement and proof, with frozen references, so the witness proof is the original one and closed over the frozen degree; they are proof support, not rows. The two least naturals are compared through eq_ex_minn (they are not convertible); no equality of proofs is claimed. hg_restrict and hg_skeleton stay shared and unchanged. X225 keeps ck before the pattern, K after its three guards, both exponents and the eventual inequality. M1 edge-set aliases and every other helper stay live in the per-row copies (no pre-M1 claim). Packaging: ClassicalLemmas maps GTBase (MathComp-only incidence.v), the root Makefile has classical-lemmas: base, and only edeg_setD's unfolding script changed; all classical, Packing and Hypergraph theorem types are unchanged."
  ],
